Introduction
1
In the complex world of military life, deployments are, without
a doubt, one of the most challenging seasons for any marriage.
It's a period of upheaval, marked by long separations,
emotional rollercoasters, and the logistical intricacies of
managing a home front single-handedly. While there are
countless books and resources aimed at helping you survive
this ordeal, this book endeavors to go one step further: to help
you and your spouse thrive, using the framework of the Five
Voices as a compass.
The Five Voices—Nurturer, Creative, Guardian, Connector, and
Pioneer—are not just mere classifications; they're insightful
lenses through which you can understand the essence of your
spouse, and, crucially, your own. Each voice possesses unique
strengths, weaknesses, and needs, providing a rich tapestry of
perspectives that can make your relationship more resilient,
more understanding, and more loving—even when thousands of
miles apart.
So, whether you're the one donning the uniform or the one
holding down the fort at home, understanding your voice and
your spouse's voice will offer you a nuanced map through the
territory of separation and reunion. From pre-deployment
preparation to the intricacies of reintegration, this guide lays
down practical, actionable steps tailored to each voice. It's not
just about enduring the deployment but about strengthening
the marital bond through it.
Now, you might think this sounds too theoretical, too abstract
for the gritty reality of military life. But understand this: Every
successful operation requires good intelligence and impeccable
communication. And what is marriage, if not the most critical,
lifelong operation you'll ever commit to?

2
So, grab a cup of coffee or tea, find a quiet space, and let's
embark on this journey. A journey that promises not only to
equip you with the tools to navigate deployments but to enrich
your marriage in ways you didn’t know were possible. 
 It's time to let the Five Voices guide you home, no matter how
far apart you are.
Introduction

Navigating Deployments Using The Five Voices
3
As a companion to the foundational principles laid out in the
Five Voices book, this guide aims to narrow the focus onto the
unique challenges and opportunities presented by
deployments in military marriages. If you haven't yet identified
your voice or that of your spouse, you'll benefit from quickly
doing so. Whether it's through the book, workshops, or online
assessments, pinning down your primary and secondary voices
provides the backdrop to understand the dynamics in play
during a deployment. Knowing your voice, and that of your
spouse, isn’t just an intellectual exercise. It is, essentially,
unlocking a code to your marriage, a decoder ring, if you will, in
a time when clear communication and understanding are
invaluable assets. 
Understanding Deployment through the Lens of Five Voices
Deployments pull no punches. They expose vulnerabilities,
stress points, and emotional reserves in ways that few other life
experiences can. Yet, each voice reacts to these common
stressors differently. Nurturers may find the absence of
physical closeness and the inability to care in person taxing.
Creatives will struggle with the rigidity of military protocols.
Guardians could fixate on the logistics and security of the
home, while Connectors may feel the sting of social isolation.
Pioneers? They'll itch for actionable strategies to close the
distance gap. These aren’t judgments; they are realities—
realities that, once understood, can be maneuvered for the
betterment of the relationship. 
For the deployed and the ones holding down the home front
alike, comprehending the implications of each voice during
deployment equips you with the savvy to adapt, respond, and
grow together—even when you're worlds apart. For instance, a
Connector will know to schedule regular video calls to maintain

Navigating Deployments Using The Five Voices
4
emotional intimacy, while a Guardian may meticulously update
a shared document with household budgets and children’s
schedules. These aren't just coping mechanisms; they are ways
to live out your marriage vows when tested by the unique
circumstances of military life. 
In this guide, you'll find insights tailored to each voice for every
stage of deployment: pre-deployment, the duration, and
reintegration. And remember, these aren't one-size-fits-all
directives but carefully tailored advice designed to help you
and your spouse not merely weather the deployment but come
out stronger on the other side.
So, while the principles behind the Five Voices remain constant,
their applications, especially in a context as challenging as
military deployments, are as varied as they are insightful. We’re
not merely surviving here; we're strategizing for success. In the
military, we adapt and overcome. In marriage, let's commit to
thriving together as a family, regardless of the miles that may
lie in between.

My Spouse is a Nurturer
5

6
Do: Establish a "heart talk" schedule to discuss emotions
openly before deployment.
Do: Make a list of emergency contacts and go-to people for
emotional support.
Do: Set up a joint calendar for bill payments and other
responsibilities.
Avoid: Making assumptions about emotional well-being.
Avoid: Leaving any financial or logistical planning for the
last minute.
Avoid: Neglecting to discuss and document the logistical
aspects of running the home.
Nurturers, often the backbone of relationships, bring emotional
depth and a keen sense for the needs of their loved ones. In the
context of a military deployment, this empathetic voice tends
to understand the emotional requirements of the situation
intuitively. Their unique ability to foster a nurturing home
environment becomes an anchor, but it also burdens them with
unspoken expectations and unsaid needs. Both spouses must
understand this duality and work collectively to navigate it.
Pre-Deployment: Setting Emotional and Practical Foundations
The period leading up to deployment can be emotionally
charged. Nurturers are likely to absorb the emotional climate,
making it crucial to establish open communication channels.
Discuss logistical matters and your anticipations, fears, and
emotional cues. Creating a pre-deployment routine can offer
comfort and predictability, helping both parties feel secure
before the looming separation.
Tips:
During Deployment: Sustaining Connection and Comfort
Deployments bring about a sea of emotions and challenges,
amplified by physical distance. For Nurturers, it's a time of
