My Spouse is a Creative

11
If your spouse is the Creative, then you've signed on for a
journey that's anything but ordinary. A Creative spouse doesn't
just ride along life's ups and downs—they examine them, turn
them inside out, and then imagine how to make them better.
Military deployment is the kind of impactful experience that a
Creative will deeply reflect on. For them, it's a chapter in an
ongoing narrative that's continuously evolving. This voice
needs a partner who can venture deep into this thoughtful
terrain, one who can appreciate the complexity yet remain
grounded in reality. You'll be the one to help make sense of
their vast world of ideas, especially during a time as significant
as a deployment.
Pre-Deployment: Setting Emotional and Practical Foundations
In the months or weeks leading up to deployment, your
Creative spouse is already living a dozen different scenarios in
their head. They're imagining what could go wrong and also
what could go incredibly right. While you're worrying about
logistics and saying your goodbyes, they're engrossed in a
world of future opportunities and latent risks. This foresight
can be invaluable, but it can also be a point of tension. It's
essential to make time for discussions that transform these
future scenarios into a tangible plan, while also leaving room
for spontaneity and discovery.
Given that they're already ten steps ahead, use this as an
advantage. Sit down together and plot out different facets of
the deployment, not just logistical but also emotional. How will
you both cope with various challenges? Where do you see
opportunities for growth? Answering these questions now can
preempt stress later.
My Spouse is a Creative

12
Do: Designate a "future-talk" day each week to convert
abstract ideas into actionable steps.
Avoid: Dismissing their far-off worries as unrealistic. They're
already considering contingencies; guide that
thoughtfulness into practical planning.
Do: Create a shared online calendar with reminders about
key dates that will require special emotional or logistical
preparation.
Avoid: Making unilateral decisions that will affect both of
you. Creative types need to feel part of the decision-making
process.
Do: Spend some time doing activities that fuel their
creativity. This helps ease stress and opens up deeper
conversations.
Avoid: Overplanning to the point where there's no space for
spontaneity. A creative mind needs room to breathe.
Pre-Deployment Tips:
During Deployment: Sustaining Connection and Comfort
During deployment, your Creative spouse will still be in their
reflective bubble. They'll miss you, yes, but they'll also ponder
the nature of absence and longing. While you may want a
straightforward update, they might deliver a philosophical
musing. It's their way of dealing with the unique pressures of a
life separated by oceans, time zones, or combat lines. This
mental space they reside in doesn't lessen their care for you;
it’s just their process, and it needs your understanding.
With a Creative, "out of sight, out of mind" doesn't apply.
Instead, the physical separation might make them cling more to
the idea of you, holding close every conversation, photograph,
or shared dream. They'll turn these into mental treasures. So,
it's vital that your communications, even when they’re short,
should be rich in quality and meaning.
My Spouse is a Creative

13
Do: Send each other curated playlists that express thoughts
and feelings words can't capture.
 Avoid: Becoming complacent with just checking boxes in
communication. Take time for one meaningful conversation
each week.
Do: Create a shared Google Doc to write long-form letters to
each other, taking turns whenever you feel inspired.
Avoid: Monopolizing conversations with day-to-day
logistics. Make room for deeper, more conceptual
discussions.
Do: Create a joint “memory bank” online where you both
upload photos, videos, and other mementos during the
separation.
Avoid: Keeping conversations strictly serious; inject some
humor or whimsy to lighten the mood.
During Deployment Tips:
Post-Deployment: Reintegration and Emotional Support
Reintegration is like turning a page, but for a Creative, it's more
like beginning a new chapter with its own twists and potentials.
The ideal situation for them is not to pick up exactly where you
left off, but to improve upon it, in a way that makes the whole
story better. Your spouse will likely have dozens of ideas on
how to do this, and it’s not just about being 'back'; it’s about
being 'better.'
While you both navigate the complicated process of settling
back into 'normal life,' remember that for a Creative, 'normal' is
ever-changing. They thrive in an environment that's constantly
evolving, so be open to discussing new routines, life goals, and
even the dynamic of your relationship itself.
My Spouse is a Creative

14
Do: Set aside a weekend for a 'vision board' session to
reimagine your post-deployment life.
Avoid: Resorting to old habits without questioning if they
still serve your evolving needs.
Do: Revisit your emotional and logistical plans to assess
what worked and what didn’t, and plan accordingly for the
future.
Avoid: Expecting to seamlessly pick up where you left off.
Take time to rediscover each other.
Do: Choose a shared project or hobby to focus on together.
It's a way to synchronize your energies.
Avoid: Filling your days with errands and chores
immediately upon return. Give yourselves time to just 'be'
together.
2. Weekly Thought-Exchange: Send each other a thought-
provoking article or book excerpt weekly. Not just for reading
but for mutual reflection.
3. Virtual Date Nights: Plan virtual dates that go beyond mere
talk. Watch a documentary or artistic film together online and
discuss it afterward.
4. Spontaneous Emotional Check-ins: Send a voice memo or
quick message just to say "I'm thinking of you," without the
need for a prolonged conversation.
Post-Deployment Tips:
Tips to Stay Connected During Deployment
1. Considering the Creative's penchant for unique experiences,
you could engage in a cooperative online puzzle or mystery
game that you both can solve together.
My Spouse is a Creative

15
5. Thematic Photo Sharing: Choose a theme for the week and
share photos related to it. It's a fun way to remain engaged and
creatively connected.
6. DIY Care Packages: Each of you could create a digital care
package: a folder of photos, quotes, or little recordings that the
other can revisit when they need comfort.
Conclusion
Being married to a Creative is like having a resident visionary in
your life. Deployment is a disruption, yes, but for a Creative, it
can be a launchpad for transformative changes that could
benefit both of you. And while the mental and emotional
landscapes of a deployment can be complex, remember that in
this complexity lie opportunities—opportunities to deepen your
relationship, to grow individually, and to come back together
stronger and wiser. So even when you're apart, you're
journeying forward together in a shared vision of what could be.
My Spouse is a Creative

