My Spouse is a Connector

22
Do: Establish a shared digital calendar to plot out both
logistical tasks and scheduled times for emotional check-
ins.
Do: Utilize apps or services that allow you to 'drop a pin' in
your location, offering your Connector spouse a
geographical touchstone to you.
Do: Create a 'Countdown Ritual' to emotionally prepare for
the time apart, like a series of special date nights or
handwritten notes.
Avoid: Leaving your communication schedule to
spontaneity. This ambiguity can be unsettling.
Avoid: Neglecting to discuss and document the logistical
aspects of running the home. This would create undue
stress.
If you're married to a Connector, you're well-acquainted with
their innate ability to form relationships and their desire to
keep the emotional pulse of their social networks. In a military
context, where deployments are a part of life, understanding
this deep-seated need for social connection can be
instrumental. Whether you are the one being deployed or the
one left behind, grasping the unique features of a Connector's
communication and relational style can aid in preserving the
marital bond through the trials of distance and time.
Pre-Deployment: Setting Emotional and Practical Foundations
In a military marriage with a Connector, pre-deployment is the
time to solidify not just logistical details but also emotional
commitments. Being clear about emotional needs and
preferences is as crucial as sorting out bills or childcare duties.
Lay down specific plans, such as scheduled phone calls or
regular email updates, that can help maintain the relationship’s
emotional fabric.
Tips:
My Spouse is a Connector

23
Avoid: Ignoring your Connector spouse's need for
affirmation and emotional clarity before your departure.
Do: Create digital 'care packages'—a collection of photos,
favorite songs, or even short voice recordings—that can be
sent periodically.
Do: Make use of social media platforms that both of you are
comfortable with to share public affirmations or mutual
memories.
Do: Designate a mutual contact as an 'emotional relay'—
someone who can convey sentiments or updates when
direct communication is challenging.
Avoid: Allowing long periods of silence to stretch between
you. This can be misinterpreted as emotional distance.
Avoid: Disregarding your pre-deployment plans without
discussing alterations first.
Avoid: Not recognizing and affirming the Connector's social
efforts, whether it's maintaining morale at home or building
camaraderie overseas.
During Deployment: Sustaining Connection and Comfort
Keeping the emotional tether strong is especially important
during the deployment phase. Your Connector spouse will
value frequent and meaningful interactions. This is the period
where you enact the communication and emotional support
plans you laid out before deployment.
Tips:
Post-Deployment: Reintegration and Emotional Support
After months of separation, the Connector will be eager to
regain not just emotional but also social stability. Reintegration
isn't just about 'picking up where you left off'; it's about
recognizing the ways both of you have grown and adapted
during the time apart.
My Spouse is a Connector

24
Do: Set aside dedicated time for open dialogues about each
other's experiences during the deployment.
Do: Coordinate a 'Welcome Back Gathering' that brings
together the various circles of friends and family your
Connector spouse cherishes.
Do: Re-establish domestic routines together, making room
for new habits formed during the deployment.
Avoid: Assuming reintegration is solely about logistical
readjustment.
Avoid: Neglecting to set new emotional expectations and
relationship boundaries.
Avoid: Dismissing or undermining your Connector spouse’s
need to process the deployment period socially as well as
emotionally.
Tips:
Tips to Stay Connected During Deployment
1.
Voice Messages: Regularly sending voice messages can
provide a sense of proximity that texts or emails often fail to
deliver. It can make your Connector spouse feel like you are
there with them, sharing real-time updates or even just random
thoughts.
2.
Virtual Sightseeing: With the help of apps and services, you
can take your spouse on virtual tours of your deployment
location or vice versa. This can provide a shared experience,
albeit digitally.
3.
Monthly Recaps: Sending a comprehensive monthly recap
can serve as a deeper, reflective connection point. This can
include highs, lows, and notable moments that may not make it
into daily or weekly conversations.
My Spouse is a Connector

25
4. Streaming Together: Thanks to technology, you can watch
the same movies or shows almost synchronously. It can make
for excellent conversation starters and provide a sense of
normality.
5. Interactive Playlist Sharing: Create a shared music playlist
where both of you can add songs that resonate with your
current mood or situation. It's a dynamic, non-verbal way to
communicate feelings or reminisce about shared memories.
6. Weekly Highlight Reel: Capture short video clips or photos
throughout the week—of places you've been, meals you've
eaten, or people you've met. Compile these into a brief
highlight reel to share during your regular video calls. It gives a
more visual and real-time feel to your experiences, making the
Connector spouse feel more involved in your life, even from a
distance.
Conclusion
Living a military life married to a Connector has its own unique
challenges and rewards. The emotional and social vibrancy they
bring can be both uplifting and complex. But like any successful
operation, the key is understanding the terrain—in this case, the
emotional and social needs of your Connector spouse. Just as
you wouldn't go into a mission without a solid plan, steering
through the deployment phases without a tailored strategy
would be a disservice to the relational richness a Connector
offers. Equip yourself with these actionable strategies, and
